## Verdict: ADVISE

The framing is useful, but both designs need corrected event, trust and interactive-lifecycle contracts before implementation.

### Blockers

- **F28-1** `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:48`, `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:50`, `crates/c3/src/consult/detached.rs:274`, `crates/c3/src/consult/detached.rs:356`, `crates/c3/src/consult/detached.rs:370`, `crates/c3/src/consult/detach.rs:60` - A wall-clock/mtime watch over status files and worker tails is not a sound change detector: it can report nothing for a dead detached background, for a foreign-host run whose liveness cannot be judged, or when an atomic terminal write lands after the read. Existing status judgement already derives died and elsewhere from pid, start time and host, so the watch must reuse that judgement and use a monotonic sequence cursor rather than timestamps. Verify: Start a detached run, kill its child out of band, and compare an mtime-only watch, a journal-based watch and `c3 consult --status`; the accepted watch must report died or unknown, never nothing. Remedy: Add one append-only closed-vocabulary event journal per task, with a sequence number and writer/run id; make every status, member, health and judgement transition append to it; have watch consume `--since <seq>` and re-run liveness judgement on open runs, returning unknown on unreadable or foreign-host records.
- **F28-5** `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:55`, `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:57`, `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:68` - Making a signed catalogue supply WASM adapters to the extension is not a viable primary form under current Chrome MV3 policy: remote WASM is remote-hosted code, and extension-page WASM requires the explicitly permitted `wasm-unsafe-eval` CSP. Adapter placement in C3 is therefore a feasibility constraint, not merely a preference. Verify: Load a scratch unpacked MV3 extension and attempt bundled and fetched WebAssembly instantiation in an extension page, content script and offscreen document; record console/CSP results. Remedy: Adopt B': adapters run as locally verified WASM in C3; if any WASM ever belongs to the extension, bundle it in the extension build and declare the minimum CSP, with catalogue updates shipped as extension updates.
- **F28-7** `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:55`, `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:59`, `crates/c3/src/engines/subprocess.rs:39`, `crates/c3/src/engines/subprocess.rs:54`, `crates/c3/src/engines/subprocess.rs:59` - A browser reviewer cannot honestly implement the current autonomous subprocess contract: timeout, stall and tree-kill assume a killable child, while draft-only's dominant state is waiting for a human and a tab or relay ending may abandon a stream. Without an interactive persisted state machine and terminal classes, `--wait`, `--kick`, budget stop and crash recovery are undefined. Verify: Walk unsent, sent, tab-closed, relay-lost, truncated-reply and operator-stop endings; each must write exactly one durable terminal record and return from wait without killing the user's tab. Remedy: Define and persist browser-specific states and terminal outcomes, pause automatic stall while awaiting user send, impose consent expiry, salvage partials, implement abandon-with-record rather than kill, and disallow unattended detached or panel seating by default.
- **F28-8** `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:57`, `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:58`, `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/02-claude-watch-and-browser-engine.md:61` - The signed catalogue and dumb bridge have no complete trust boundary: the signer, key rotation, revocation, extension/relay update pinning, adapter content identity and per-operation capability scope are unspecified. A table keyed only by adapter name plus vendor also re-labels history when an adapter changes, and a generic DOM bridge can drive or read beyond the catalogued vendor scope. Verify: Write the bridge message specification and execute one out-of-scope origin/tab operation and one revoked or updated adapter hash; both must be refused, and intake rows must distinguish hashes. Remedy: Name a trust root and revocation mechanism; key catalogue entries, consent and telemetry by adapter content hash plus catalogue revision; bind each bridge operation to a per-run capability token, adapter hash, exact origin and tab; pin and audit extension updates.
- **F21-3** (prior, still-open) `crates/c3/src/engines/codex.rs:1` - A browser reviewer cannot satisfy the engine contract the sketch promises (brief in, structured reply out, transcript, kill record): there is no child to kill, and the dominant state 'draft prepared, user has not pressed send' has no timeout, no partial, and no owner in the current recovery chain.

### Unproven scenarios

- Effective Claude Code prompt-cache lifetime and cached-input savings from watch-first wakes.
- Real SubagentStart/Stop payload fields and worker STATE formats across resumes and crashes.
- Actual Chrome WASM/CSP behavior in every relevant extension context on the installed Chrome version.
- Native messaging concurrency, Windows host lifecycle and extension service-worker behavior with multiple simultaneous C3 runs.
- Vendor composer autosave behavior, read-scope enforcement and terms of service for logged-in web chats.

